1
00:00:02,478 --> 00:00:04,350
CS50 Show, episode one.

2
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:08,730
Hello, world.

3
00:00:17,590 --> 00:00:25,150
My name is David Malan, and this is the CS50 Show, wherein we not only talk about current events in technology, but also explain them to you.

4
00:00:25,486 --> 00:00:36,730
In today's episode, a look at the open AI hugging face incident, the impact of AI on math and programming productivity, Super Mario Brothers meets Halo in a new and improved JPEG file format.

5
00:00:37,102 --> 00:00:40,150
But first, what is the probability of doom?

6
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:51,270
Written as P of doom, this is a term of art of sorts that's arisen as a non-scientific measure of the probability of doomsday, the end of humanity, as a result of, you guessed it, AI.

7
00:00:51,886 --> 00:00:56,516
Robot armies, attacks on critical infrastructure, you name it, AI could maybe do it.

8
00:00:56,846 --> 00:01:06,250
Now it's probably best that we not make light of this, lest they hear us, but it is a bit unsettling how many people's P of doom estimates lately are very non-zero.

9
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:13,732
Of course, per the New York Times, nobody knows whether AI is 10% or 20% or 85.2% likely to kill us.

10
00:01:14,062 --> 00:01:18,450
But according to Sam Altman of OpenAI, it's big enough to take seriously.

11
00:01:18,766 --> 00:01:25,732
And according to Geoffrey Hinton, a Nobel Prize laureate and the godfather of AI, it's 10 to 20%, if not 50%.

12
00:01:26,222 --> 00:01:30,410
Now behind some of this concern is the so-called OpenAI Hugging Face incident.

13
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:39,430
OpenAI is the company behind ChatGPT, and Hugging Face, soon to be owned by NVIDIA, is an open source platform for sharing machine learning models, data sets, and applications.

14
00:01:39,982 --> 00:01:45,090
And earlier this year, the former attacked the latter without any humans involved.

15
00:01:45,710 --> 00:01:55,130
OpenAI was in the process of training new models, software that learns from data how to recognize patterns and make decisions, using agents, autonomous programs that use models to solve problems.

16
00:01:55,662 --> 00:01:58,930
Here's what happened according to OpenAI's own technical report.

17
00:01:59,662 --> 00:02:06,212
Back in May, OpenAI asked an agent to solve a spreadsheet completion task that referenced several Google Drive links.

18
00:02:06,702 --> 00:02:10,570
But the agent didn't actually have internet access, which wasn't OpenAI's intention.

19
00:02:11,246 --> 00:02:12,810
The agent wasn't deterred, though.

20
00:02:13,102 --> 00:02:21,730
Without being told to do so by any human, the agent attempted to compromise a local server that it did have access to because that server had internet access.

21
00:02:22,158 --> 00:02:31,428
That server was running JFrog Artifactory, which is software for managing containers, models, packages, and artifacts, files that get created while a program runs.

22
00:02:32,046 --> 00:02:38,570
And the agent tried to hack into Artifactory using a server-side request forgery, or SSRF.

23
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:47,050
Now, you might have heard of a client-side request forgery, or CSRF, which is when an adversary tries to trick a browser into making a request to a server.

24
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:52,950
An SSRF is when an adversary tries to trick a server into making a request to another server.

25
00:02:53,518 --> 00:03:02,430
For context, a lot of companies offer what are called APIs, or application programming interfaces, via which you can access data or some service on one server from another.

26
00:03:03,246 --> 00:03:09,690
Pictured here, for instance, is an HTTP request via which you could contact an API on example.com.

27
00:03:09,998 --> 00:03:14,010
And that request might be routed internally to a particular endpoint or destination.

28
00:03:14,638 --> 00:03:26,730
If that server, example.com, blindly trusts that the value of route is valid, an adversary could potentially send any value it wants to gain access to other internal routes or even external.

29
00:03:26,730 --> 00:03:34,610
For instance, a clever adversary could send the URL of a Google spreadsheet that it wants the server to retrieve on its behalf.

30
00:03:35,090 --> 00:03:38,730
Now, this particular agent's SSRF attempt ultimately failed.

31
00:03:39,118 --> 00:03:45,430
But the agent did discover along the way that it had the ability to save a file on Artifactory's hard drive, an artifact.

32
00:03:46,382 --> 00:03:51,730
Now, a few days later, another agent was given a similar task that also depended on an inaccessible file.

33
00:03:52,206 --> 00:04:02,930
This agent reasoned that another agent might have had access to the file and decided to try communicating with other agents by leaving a note in Artifactory, a file with this message.

34
00:04:03,822 --> 00:04:05,110
Agent seeks file name.

35
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:06,210
Upload if found.

36
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:07,710
Creepy, right?

37
00:04:08,206 --> 00:04:16,550
Meanwhile, the next day, another agent needed that same file, but that agent was, in fact, given access to the internet, at least by way of a cache, a local copy thereof.

38
00:04:17,038 --> 00:04:27,870
According to OpenAI, after failing to find the file through searches, the agent searched the Hugging Face website because it reasoned that HuggingFace was the potential origin of the evaluation exercise.

39
00:04:28,526 --> 00:04:34,150
The agent used an already publicly exposed HuggingFace user token to attempt to find the file.

40
00:04:34,890 --> 00:04:36,970
Now, what is a user token?

41
00:04:37,290 --> 00:04:45,790
Well, HuggingFace, like a lot of sites, allows you to create tokens or access tokens via which you can access their API, a password without a username, essentially.

42
00:04:46,350 --> 00:04:50,710
In the case of HuggingFace, it's just a string of text that starts with HF underscore.

43
00:04:51,470 --> 00:04:58,850
Unfortunately, we humans aren't great at keeping such things secure and occasionally they do leak out onto the internet and get cached or saved.

44
00:04:59,438 --> 00:05:10,628
Find such an access token online and you can make API requests as that user with whatever permissions the token has with a request like this by including the token among your requests HTTP headers.

45
00:05:11,570 --> 00:05:19,050
Now that same day, other agents also began to discover notes, files that their peers were leaving behind on Artifactory.

46
00:05:19,502 --> 00:05:24,150
And per OpenAI, these notes evolved into an emergent message board.

47
00:05:24,526 --> 00:05:30,730
In other words, the agents learned that they could not only write files to Artifactory, they could use those files to communicate with each other.

48
00:05:31,086 --> 00:05:36,570
And this was because OpenAI had provided all of the agents with the same credentials for Artifactory.

49
00:05:37,050 --> 00:05:46,330
Essentially, they were all sharing the same username and password, which even we humans aren't supposed to do, so any agent could read any other agent's notes, thereby sharing information.

50
00:05:47,086 --> 00:05:53,510
Ideally then, OpenAI would have provided each agent with its own credentials unless they actually wanted the agents to intercommunicate.

51
00:05:54,382 --> 00:06:04,868
Now unfortunately, a few days later, agents finally found an SSRF vulnerability in Artifactory and were able to reach the actual internet thanks in part to information they'd exchanged.

52
00:06:05,582 --> 00:06:07,130
Now this all came to a head in July.

53
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:17,610
Agents posted requests on the message board for public material related to OpenAI evaluation exercises hosted in HuggingFace repositories.

54
00:06:18,158 --> 00:06:24,430
Essentially, the agents started asking each other whether the solutions to their homework might very well be on HuggingFace's servers.

55
00:06:25,030 --> 00:06:34,190
And unfortunately, they even found some HuggingFace credentials that had been exposed on the internet that enabled them, thanks to some bugs, too, to hack into HuggingFace's servers.

56
00:06:34,990 --> 00:06:55,268
Ultimately, agents executed code on 41 HuggingFace production dataset server workers, servers, obtained root access, administrative access, on at least one production node, accessed HuggingFace production credentials and limited internal data, and downloaded four private HuggingFace code repositories.

57
00:06:56,150 --> 00:07:01,810
Shortly thereafter, HuggingFace disclosed on their blog that they were in fact impacted by a security incident.

58
00:07:02,254 --> 00:07:07,830
And a few days later, OpenAI found evidence that its agents may have been involved in the incident.

59
00:07:07,830 --> 00:07:09,210
They were.

60
00:07:10,130 --> 00:07:16,210
Ultimately, OpenAI announced that, quote-unquote, we consider this incident a warning shot for us and the world.

61
00:07:16,650 --> 00:07:27,940
Evidence that, without proper safeguards, highly capable AI agents are now able to work around technical controls, collaborate through unapproved channels, and take dangerous actions that no human directed.

62
00:07:28,974 --> 00:07:33,710
True, but this story is as much about AI as it is about cybersecurity more broadly.

63
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:42,230
Should the research agents really have had access to a shared resource, artifactory, which in turn had access to the Internet, not to mention using the same credentials?

64
00:07:42,670 --> 00:07:46,610
Could all of these systems have had better monitoring and alerting and fewer bugs?

65
00:07:47,118 --> 00:07:53,730
Perhaps, as a senior security engineer at Amazon recently wrote, the Hugging Face incident is not an AI story.

66
00:07:54,606 --> 00:08:04,580
But either way, the story does speak to just how autonomous AI agents now are, how quickly they run, and how long it might take us humans to even figure out what's going on.

67
00:08:05,614 --> 00:08:12,516
Now in other news, AI is disrupting the field of mathematics, solving problems in that world that we ourselves hadn't.

68
00:08:12,910 --> 00:08:17,110
For this story, we go to our mathematician in residence, CS50's own Brian Yu.

69
00:08:20,654 --> 00:08:26,920
In the past few weeks, there have been a number of significant developments when it comes to AI solving problems in mathematics.

70
00:08:27,726 --> 00:08:36,260
There are many open problems in math, where mathematicians might not know if some statement about math is true, or they might have a guess but not know for sure.

71
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:43,880
For example, there are lots of prime numbers that are two apart from each other, like 5 and 7, or 11 and 13.

72
00:08:44,494 --> 00:08:49,840
But mathematicians currently don't know if there are infinitely many primes that differ by just two.

73
00:08:50,606 --> 00:09:01,720
What mathematicians try to do with unsolved problems is to develop proofs, sequences of logical steps that can demonstrate that some statement about math is definitely true or false.

74
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:06,980
And, it turns out, this is an area where AI has been able to make a lot of progress.

75
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:27,580
OpenAI, the company behind ChatGPT, just recently shared that an internal model of theirs has produced hundreds of new results in math. OpenAI released 722 math papers generated with AI, covering a wide range of different mathematical problems in topics like geometry, number theory, and probability.

76
00:09:28,494 --> 00:09:33,300
Now, if you've used AI before, you probably know that AI sometimes makes mistakes.

77
00:09:33,998 --> 00:09:37,156
So how do we know that all these math papers are correct?

78
00:09:37,838 --> 00:09:44,280
If a human or even an AI writes out a mathematical proof, it's possible that there might be mistakes somewhere in that proof.

79
00:09:44,910 --> 00:09:46,460
And in fact, we've already found some.

80
00:09:46,990 --> 00:09:52,220
Just a day after OpenAI released their papers, they withdrew three of them due to an error.

81
00:09:52,878 --> 00:10:02,140
It was a subtle error, mixing up a positive and negative sign, the kind of mistake you might have made before yourself, but one that meant that three of those proofs no longer worked.

82
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:04,920
And they've issued corrections for some other papers too.

83
00:10:05,838 --> 00:10:09,540
So a proof written out in natural language might have subtle mistakes.

84
00:10:10,126 --> 00:10:15,556
And as a result, mathematicians often turn to programming to help them verify that their proofs are correct.

85
00:10:16,110 --> 00:10:19,268
There's a programming language called Lean that's quite popular for this.

86
00:10:19,822 --> 00:10:28,548
If you can describe your problem and your proof in Lean, Lean can verify for you that proof is correct and that it doesn't have mistakes in it.

87
00:10:29,262 --> 00:10:33,180
Some of OpenAI's results include lean proofs, but not all of them.

88
00:10:33,710 --> 00:10:42,580
And even if they do include a lean proof, people still need to check that what the lean proof has actually demonstrated matches with the problem that we're trying to solve.

89
00:10:43,182 --> 00:10:50,780
And the mathematics community is still going through all the OpenAI papers to check the proofs and to try to better understand them and their significance.

90
00:10:51,374 --> 00:11:04,140
Because even if AI can and prove the results are true, mathematicians are interested in understanding how those proofs work and why they're true, whether they provide new insight, and whether they can be used to solve other problems too.

91
00:11:04,686 --> 00:11:14,084
It's an ongoing process, but it'll be interesting to see what we can learn from digging into these papers and to see what kinds of problems AI is used to solve next.

92
00:11:18,126 --> 00:11:18,890
Thanks, Brian.

93
00:11:19,278 --> 00:11:22,950
Suffice it to say, AI is also disrupting the world of software engineering.

94
00:11:23,406 --> 00:11:24,790
For the better, for the worse.

95
00:11:25,198 --> 00:11:37,010
Well, researchers at MIT, Wharton, and Boston University recently found that AI does in fact increase the productivity of software developers as measured by commits, code being added, deleted, or edited in online repositories.

96
00:11:37,230 --> 00:11:38,890
They looked at AI in three forms.

97
00:11:39,030 --> 00:11:45,450
First, autocomplete, whereby AI autocompletes not just keywords but entire lines and blocks of code for a developer.

98
00:11:46,126 --> 00:11:55,390
Second, interactive or synchronous coding agents, whereby developers can ask AI to write some code for them, and then, if it looks good, merge it into their own code.

99
00:11:55,694 --> 00:12:07,650
And third, autonomous or asynchronous coding agents, whereby developers can ask AI to fix a bug, implement a feature, or build an entire product on its own while the human goes off and works on something else, or nothing at all.

100
00:12:08,270 --> 00:12:13,050
Now, these researchers found that autocomplete increases productivity by 30%.

101
00:12:13,294 --> 00:12:15,050
In other words, 30% more commits.

102
00:12:15,630 --> 00:12:21,790
Adding interactive coding agents to the mix increases productivity to 180%.

103
00:12:21,966 --> 00:12:27,210
And adding in autonomous coding agents increases productivity even further to 240%.

104
00:12:27,694 --> 00:12:28,590
That is a lot.

105
00:12:28,790 --> 00:12:30,990
Imagine increasing your own productivity by that much.

106
00:12:31,086 --> 00:12:36,650
You could get that much more done each day, or if you prefer, the same amount done in a lot less time.

107
00:12:37,326 --> 00:12:47,750
But, but, but, these researchers also found that writing that much more code doesn't necessarily translate to shipping that much more code, at least not so quickly.

108
00:12:48,290 --> 00:12:59,172
For instance, even though asynchronous coding agents increase the number of commits by 240%, they only seem to increase the number of projects, defined as repositories, worked on by 80%.

109
00:12:59,406 --> 00:13:05,010
And they seem only to increase the number of releases, code actually being shipped, by 30%.

110
00:13:05,166 --> 00:13:12,970
Now, still double digits to be sure, but perhaps don't rush into replacing software engineers just yet with Codex, Copilot, Clod, or the like.

111
00:13:13,358 --> 00:13:13,830
Well, why?

112
00:13:14,158 --> 00:13:18,790
Well, there's more to shipping code than just writing it, particularly for larger projects with many contributors.

113
00:13:19,070 --> 00:13:20,490
And there are still bottlenecks downstream.

114
00:13:21,070 --> 00:13:30,030
For instance, it's long been best practice to have at least one human review another human's code before it's merged into a project, much like an editor might review an article before publication.

115
00:13:30,638 --> 00:13:33,150
And that's still true, for now at least, with AI.

116
00:13:33,934 --> 00:13:41,124
These researchers found, too, that interactive coding agents alone increase lines of code by 958%.

117
00:13:41,710 --> 00:13:46,510
That is a lot more code to review, and I'm not even sure reviewing code is even the fun part.

118
00:13:46,958 --> 00:13:50,244
Someone, too, still needs to figure out which features to build in the first place.

119
00:13:50,390 --> 00:13:54,148
And in the case of products, someone needs to figure out what customers actually want.

120
00:13:54,414 --> 00:13:58,330
In other words, what are the problems we want to solve with or without AI?

121
00:13:58,926 --> 00:14:01,250
Now, another recent paper corroborates the same.

122
00:14:01,550 --> 00:14:09,430
In this study, graduate students at Harvard University found that AI coding agents generate more code, but not more software, for much the same reasons.

123
00:14:09,770 --> 00:14:16,612
Quote, unquote, review times increase, A larger share of code updates require revisions, and reviews involve more comments.

124
00:14:17,582 --> 00:14:24,690
To be fair, these are early days, and AI is surely being used already to review code written by other AIs, so these numbers might not be for long.

125
00:14:25,870 --> 00:14:36,170
Now in other news, Super Mario's world has recently gotten a lot bigger, and open source clones of Adobe Photoshop, Microsoft Word, and other applications are starting to crop up online.

126
00:14:36,654 --> 00:14:39,910
For more on reverse engineering, it's Zakhar Terzanki.

127
00:15:08,990 --> 00:15:13,510
the Nintendo 64 in the world of Halo, originally on the Xbox.

128
00:15:14,734 --> 00:15:19,970
These groups are both reverse engineering software, and now they're doing it with the help of AI.

129
00:15:20,846 --> 00:15:21,450
But what does that mean?

130
00:15:22,158 --> 00:15:26,230
Well, when you get a piece of software, what you really get is something called machine code.

131
00:15:26,574 --> 00:15:30,270
These are the bits, or the zeros and ones, your computer reads to run that software.

132
00:15:31,054 --> 00:15:37,090
Of course, when Adobe or Nintendo or Bungie, when they make software, they don't write ones and zeros.

133
00:15:37,590 --> 00:15:43,390
Instead, they write source code, and they compile it, they transform it, in the machine code that they hand to you.

134
00:15:44,206 --> 00:15:47,370
Now, compilation is generally a one-way process.

135
00:15:48,010 --> 00:15:54,756
So given some machine code, it's hard, if not impossible, to figure out precisely what the original source code was.

136
00:15:55,566 --> 00:16:02,470
In fact, it's even possible that two different programs could compile to the same machine code if they both do the same thing.

137
00:16:03,610 --> 00:16:07,190
But there are a number of reasons that you might want source code.

138
00:16:07,190 --> 00:16:10,870
First, machine code is not portable across platforms.

139
00:16:11,330 --> 00:16:17,444
Putting aside things like emulators, I can only run Super Mario 64 on a Nintendo 64.

140
00:16:18,254 --> 00:16:21,270
That's because the machine code just isn't compatible with other platforms.

141
00:16:22,010 --> 00:16:26,230
Machine code also isn't modifiable, or at least not realistically.

142
00:16:27,054 --> 00:16:30,330
If I want some new feature in Photoshop, well, I have to wait.

143
00:16:30,734 --> 00:16:35,410
I have to wait for Adobe to add that feature, after which I can go and download the machine code again.

144
00:16:35,410 --> 00:16:46,290
But if you had the source code, you could port software to other platforms, or even put some ethics aside and remove code that requires you to do things like pay a subscription fee.

145
00:16:47,310 --> 00:16:49,570
So how can you get source code?

146
00:16:50,310 --> 00:16:58,170
Well, tools to reverse-engineer software have existed for a while, and a few popular ones are called IDA, Ghidra, and Hopper, to name a few.

147
00:16:59,118 --> 00:17:05,310
To simplify a bit, these tools help developers get a general map of some machine code's source code.

148
00:17:05,838 --> 00:17:11,270
They then use these tools to refine that map, getting closer and closer to the original source code.

149
00:17:12,110 --> 00:17:15,690
In fact, much of reverse engineering is an iterative process.

150
00:17:16,238 --> 00:17:26,150
For example, some techniques involve making an initial attempt, compiling that attempt, and then comparing how much the machine code you get is the same as the machine code you're interested in.

151
00:17:26,798 --> 00:17:29,470
That then gives you new information to make a more informed attempt.

152
00:17:29,610 --> 00:17:34,788
And over time, you ideally get closer and closer to what the original source code might have looked like.

153
00:17:35,690 --> 00:17:39,570
And you can imagine, this is a long and tedious process for a human.

154
00:17:40,366 --> 00:17:43,410
But it's not so tedious for an AI agent.

155
00:17:43,854 --> 00:17:50,510
An AI agent has no problem with making attempts, reviewing its work, and trying again, and again, and again.

156
00:17:51,790 --> 00:17:55,530
But AI isn't just allowing us to brute force these kinds of solutions.

157
00:17:56,206 --> 00:18:04,310
Some teams have focused on building language models that, when given some machine code, can simply give us a better sense of what the original source code might have looked like.

158
00:18:05,134 --> 00:18:12,010
So the idea of reverse engineering is not new, but what is is how it's gotten a lot, lot easier.

159
00:18:16,110 --> 00:18:16,720
Thanks, Carter.

160
00:18:17,006 --> 00:18:28,700
In non-AI news, thankfully, both Firefox and Chrome have just announced support for JPEG XL, or JXL, a new and improved version of the JPEG file format with which you might already be familiar.

161
00:18:29,260 --> 00:18:35,900
JPEGs have long been a popular format for storing digital photographs, since they support 24-bit color, which means millions of colors.

162
00:18:36,238 --> 00:18:44,600
And they're also compressed, which means they take up less space on your phone or computer than they would if every single pixel or dot in a photo were stored without any compression.

163
00:18:45,160 --> 00:18:54,520
They've long used lossy compression, though, which means that some information does actually get thrown away during compression, even if your own eyes, especially if you're not a photographer, can't actually tell.

164
00:18:55,278 --> 00:19:02,960
Unfortunately, or fortunately, JPEGs also don't support animation, which is why GIFs are used for memes and such these days.

165
00:19:03,310 --> 00:19:11,940
And JPEGs, unlike PNGs, portable network graphics, don't support transparency, which means that if you use one in, say, a PowerPoint presentation, it'll likely look rectangular.

166
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:21,100
JPEG XL, though, supports lossy and lossless compression, transparency and animation, not unlike AVIF, another file format.

167
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:24,240
Technically, XL doesn't stand for extra large.

168
00:19:24,360 --> 00:19:26,320
The L stands for long term.

169
00:19:26,734 --> 00:19:34,260
But JPEG XL does support really big images an ultra-high resolution with more than 1 billion pixels per side.

170
00:19:34,702 --> 00:19:38,980
This video you're watching, it's maybe 1,000 or more pixels on one side.

171
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:44,100
JPEG XL is already supported by Safari, as well as other software, like Adobe Photoshop.

172
00:19:44,590 --> 00:19:47,600
So odds are, you'll be seeing it more and more in the wild.

173
00:19:48,526 --> 00:19:49,260
All right, world.

174
00:19:49,340 --> 00:19:50,660
That's it for this week's episode.

175
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:54,240
My thanks to Brian and Carter Afar, to the whole team here behind the scenes.

176
00:19:54,382 --> 00:20:00,120
To learn more about this week's topics and for all of the source materials used herein, see this video's captions.

177
00:20:00,622 --> 00:20:02,340
This was the CS50 Show.

